# Home Assistant interface coverage

Every entry in the registries below has an assigned local component, style and task pattern, with a complete reference example. This is **design coverage of a defined source baseline**. It is not a claim that every Home Assistant integration has been redesigned, that every native control has passed browser testing, or that a documentation example installs a native adapter.

<% inventory = JSON.parse(File.read(File.join(root, 'data', 'ha-interface-coverage.json'))) %>

<h2 id="baseline">Coherent version baseline</h2>

This inventory was reviewed on 12 November 2026 against Home Assistant Core **<%= inventory['baseline']['core_version'] %>** and frontend **<%= inventory['baseline']['frontend_version'] %>**. Core's frontend manifest pins that exact frontend release. Public documentation is separately fixed to the website commit recorded in the [machine-readable inventory](../reference/ha-interface-coverage.json).

The <%= inventory['totals']['entries'] %> records belong to <%= inventory['totals']['categories'] %> overlapping registries. They are **not <%= inventory['totals']['entries'] %> unique user-visible components**: for example, a public card, its source dispatcher key and its domain control have different records. Internal routes, legacy aliases and reserved entries are labelled rather than recommended as new public configuration.

<h2 id="scope">What is included</h2>

- Every public dashboard card catalogue entry, all four native view layouts and every pinned card dispatcher key.
- Every registered card feature, every closed feature style variant, Tile placement, badges, header/footer types and picture elements.
- Every entity/special-row dispatcher key, domain-to-row mapping and dedicated More info control file.
- Every public selector, native selector dispatcher key and explicit legacy selector alias.
- All Core entity platforms; additional helper/state domains are separately limited to those referenced by the native row/dialog registries.
- Native device-control primitives and shared user-facing primitive families, with their exact source members.
- Top-level panel families, Settings routes, developer tools and native external settings destinations.

Integration-specific modes, ranges, attributes, media sources and permissions remain dynamic. A fixed inventory cannot invent their values. Arbitrary custom cards, custom More info controls, custom panels and custom integrations require a separately reviewed extension record. Source utility functions, CSS-only helpers, animation machinery and instrumentation are not independent public components.

<h2 id="interpretation">How to read a mapping</h2>

The task-pattern link specifies the relevant behaviour and uncertainty boundaries. The component link identifies a reusable visual/interaction owner; the style link supplies the governing visual roles. The example demonstrates an original household scenario. It can be a reference for a family without pretending to reproduce the native component's source or every capability variant.

Native implementation still needs an actual versioned acceptance record. Check the [compatibility register](compatibility-register.html), [native task accessibility](native-task-accessibility.html), and the [current audit evidence](../community/home-assistant-coverage-audit.html). Preserve native translations, user-supplied names, values, event handling and permissions. A theme cannot add missing application behaviour through CSS.

